      <gmd:abstract>
        <gco:CharacterString>Extreme storms can produce devastating floods, yet understanding how these storms develop and change over time remains challenging, particularly in mountainous regions where terrain strongly influences precipitation. This dataset provides a collection of detailed WRF simulations of hundreds of extreme storms sampled from the CESM2 Single Model Initial Condition Large Ensemble (SMILE) across the Sierra Nevada and Upper Colorado River Basin in the western United States. The storms were selected for their extreme precipitation, downscaled to 4 km grid spacing with 2D surface output archived at hourly time steps. Each simulation provides a detailed picture of how a storm evolves, including where and when precipitation falls, how intense it becomes, and whether it falls as rain or snow. We selected dozens of the most extreme events for 1-day, 3-day, 7-day, and 15-day accumulation intervals for the 1980-2014 reference and 2015-2049 mid 21st century periods. These storm representations, or "storm templates," provide valuable information that is often missing from global model output or statistically downscaled datasets. Researchers and water managers can use these templates to investigate how differences in storm location, intensity, duration, and precipitation type influence flooding and water availability. The dataset also supports the development and evaluation of improved methods for estimating extreme precipitation and flood hazards, helping inform long-term water resources planning, infrastructure resilience, and dam safety assessments under a range of possible future conditions.</gco:CharacterString>
      </gmd:abstract>
